   After execute_origin_dispatch, vehicle_available becomes true (good for goal). Also vehicle_allocated remains true.

   Also need origin_node_occupied and origin_node_ready: we will get via load_origin_payload (sets occupied) and activate_origin_node (sets ready). However activate_origin_node requires origin_node_occupied false, so we must activate before loading payload. So order: activate_origin_node (requires origin_node_occupied false), then load_origin_payload (makes occupied). After load, origin_node_ready stays true (not removed). So we get both ready and occupied.

   Also need origin_availability_confirmed and locked: lock_origin_availability after activation and after candidate_prepared etc.

   Also need candidate_prepared origin_availability_alpha: we need to run confirm_candidate_preparedness for origin_availability_alpha. Preconditions: candidate_created origin_availability_alpha (true), vehicle_allocated origin_availability_alpha (true), candidate_task_profile_linked origin_availability_alpha ?route_task_profile (need to attach), not candidate_prepared origin_availability_alpha (currently false). So we need to attach task profile to origin_availability_alpha first, then run confirm_candidate_preparedness.

   Also need candidate_operator_linked origin_availability_alpha ?operator: we can assign operator via assign_operator_resource (requires candidate_prepared ?candidate? Actually assign_operator_resource precondition: candidate_prepared ?candidate, operator_available ?operator_resource). So we need origin_availability_alpha prepared before assigning operator. So we need to prepare origin_availability_alpha first (via confirm_candidate_preparedness), then assign operator.

   However assign_operator_resource also requires candidate_prepared ?candidate. So we need origin_availability_alpha prepared before assigning operator.

   So order for origin:
